    <main class="lesson-content">
        <article class="lesson-article">
            <header class="lesson-header"><span class="lesson-number">Lesson 9</span><h1>Parallel.For and Parallel.ForEach</h1><div class="lesson-meta"><span class="lesson-duration"><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><polyline points="12 6 12 12 16 14"/></svg>30 min</span><span class="lesson-level intermediate">Intermediate</span></div></header>
            <div class="lesson-body">
                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>When to Use Parallel Loops</h2>
                    <p>Use Parallel loops for <strong>CPU-bound</strong> work that can be distributed across cores.</p>
                    <div class="info-box">
                        <div class="info-box-header"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12" y2="12"/><line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12.01" y2="8"/></svg><span>Key Point</span></div>
                        <p>For I/O-bound work, use async/await with Task.WhenAll instead.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Parallel.For</h2>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Sequential
for (int i = 0; i &lt; 1000; i++)
{
    ProcessItem(i);
}

// Parallel - uses multiple cores
Parallel.For(0, 1000, i =&gt;
{
    ProcessItem(i);  // Each iteration may run on different thread
});</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Parallel.ForEach</h2>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">var files = Directory.GetFiles(path, "*.jpg");

// Process all files in parallel
Parallel.ForEach(files, file =&gt;
{
    var image = LoadImage(file);
    var thumbnail = CreateThumbnail(image);
    SaveThumbnail(thumbnail, file);
});</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>ParallelOptions</h2>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">var options = new ParallelOptions
{
    MaxDegreeOfParallelism = Environment.ProcessorCount,
    CancellationToken = cancellationToken
};

try
{
    Parallel.ForEach(items, options, item =&gt;
    {
        ProcessItem(item);
    });
}
catch (OperationCanceledException)
{
    Console.WriteLine("Operation was cancelled");
}</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Parallel.ForEachAsync (.NET 6+)</h2>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// For async operations with controlled concurrency
await Parallel.ForEachAsync(urls, 
    new ParallelOptions { MaxDegreeOfParallelism = 10 },
    async (url, ct) =&gt;
    {
        var content = await httpClient.GetStringAsync(url, ct);
        await ProcessContentAsync(content);
    });</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Key Takeaways</h2>
                    <div class="takeaways">
                        <LessonTakeaway>Parallel loops are for CPU-bound work.</LessonTakeaway>
                        <LessonTakeaway>Use MaxDegreeOfParallelism to control threads.</LessonTakeaway>
                        <LessonTakeaway>Parallel.ForEachAsync combines parallel + async.</LessonTakeaway>
                    </div>
                </section>
